"The Probability That a Random Graph is Even-Decomposable (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/rsa.70034)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; Versteegen (2025) conjectured that $$$ {r}_H(n) $$$ has polynomial growth if and only if it is even-decomposable. ... (Versteegen ([2025]))$$$ {r}_H(n)={n}^{\\Omega (1)} $$$ if and only if $$$ H $$$ is even-decomposable. ... Our first result is a proof of Conjecture 1.6.\nAlon (2024a) observed that if $$$ e(H) $$$ is even, then it follows from the best bound on multicolour Ramsey numbers that $$$ {r}_H(n)=\\Omega \\left(\\log n/\\mathrm{loglog}n\\right) $$$. Improving this, Versteegen (2025) showed that for some absolute constant $$$ c $$$, we have $$$ {r}_H(n)\\ge c\\log n $$$ for all graphs $$$ H $$$ with an even number of edges. On the other hand, it was shown in (Cameron and Heath 2023) that $$$ {r}_{K_4}(n)={n}^{o(1)} $$$, and it was proved in (Ge et al. 2023) and (Bennett et al. 2023) (independently) that $$$ {r}_{K_5}(n)={n}^{o(1)} $$$. At the same time, it is easy to construct graphs for which $$$ {r}_H(n) $$$ grows polynomially. This naturally raises the following question, which was considered by Versteegen (2025).\n\nQuestion 1.1.For which graphs $$$ H $$$, with an even number of edges, is $$$ {r}_H(n)={n}^{o(1)} $$$?\n\nTo address this question, Versteegen introduced the following class of graphs.\n\nDefinition 1.2. (Even-decomposable graphs)A graph $$$ H $$$ is even-decomposable if there is a sequence $$$ V(H)={V}_0\\supset {V}_1\\supset \\cdots \\supset {V}_k=\\varnothing $$$ such that for each $$$ 0\\le i\\le k-1 $$$, $$$ H\\left[{V}_i\\right] $$$ has an even number of edges and $$$ {V}_i\\setminus {V}_{i+1} $$$ is an independent set in $$$ H $$$.\n\nNote that any even-decomposable graph has an even number of edges.\n\nDefinition 1.3. (Even-non-decomposable graphs)If a graph has an even number of edges, but is not even-decomposable, we call it even-non-decomposable.\n\nObserve that $$$ {K}_4 $$$ is even-non-decomposable. Versteegen (2025) conjectured that $$$ {r}_H(n) $$$ has polynomial growth if and only if it is even-decomposable.\n\nConjecture 1.4. (Versteegen ([2025]))$$$ {r}_H(n)={n}^{\\Omega (1)} $$$ if and only if $$$ H $$$ is even-decomposable.\n\nHe proved that if $$$ H $$$ is even-decomposable, then indeed $$$ {r}_H(n) $$$ has polynomial growth, verifying the ‘if’ direction of Conjecture 1.4.\n\nHe also showed that almost all graphs with an even number of edges are even-decomposable. Here and below, $$$ G\\left(n,p\\right) $$$ denotes the binomial random graph on $$$ n $$$ vertices with edge probability $$$ p $$$.\n\nTheorem 1.5. (Versteegen ([2025]))If $$$ H\\sim G\\left(n,\\frac{1}{2}\\right) $$$, then\n\n$$$$ \\mathbb{P}\\left(H\\kern0.3em is\\ even\\hbox{-} non\\hbox{-} decomposable\\right)={e}^{-\\Omega \\left(\\sqrt{\\log n}\\right)}. $$$$\n\nVersteegen conjectured the following much stronger bound for the proportion of even-non-decomposable graphs.\n\nConjecture 1.6. (Versteegen ([2025]))If $$$ H\\sim G\\left(n,\\frac{1}{2}\\right) $$$, then\n\n$$$$ \\mathbb{P}\\left(H\\kern0.3em is\\ even\\hbox{-} non\\hbox{-} decomposable\\right)={e}^{-\\Omega \\left({n}^2\\right)}. $$$$\n\nOur first result is a proof of Conjecture 1.6.\n\nTheorem 1.7.If $$$ H\\sim G\\left(n,\\frac{1}{2}\\right) $$$, then\n\n$$$$ \\mathbb{P}\\left(H\\kern0.3em is\\ even\\hbox{-} non\\hbox{-} decomposable\\right)={e}^{-\\Omega \\left({n}^2\\right)}. $$$$\n\nThis bound is trivially tight up to the value of the implied constant (e.g., since $$$ {K}_4 $$$ is even-non-decomposable).\n\n## 4 Concluding Remarks\n\nIn this paper, we proved (in Theorem 1.13) that the probability that $$$ G\\left(n,1/2\\right) $$$ is not even-degenerate is $$$ {e}^{-\\Theta (n)} $$$. Our argument relied on the edge probability being exactly $$$ 1/2 $$$. It would be interesting to extend this result to a general $$$ G\\left(n,p\\right) $$$, at least when $$$ p $$$ is a constant.\n\nNote added. After the first version of this paper was made available, progress on extending Theorem 1.13 to the case of constant $$$ p $$$ was made in (Chao et al. 2025).\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nWe are grateful to Noga Alon and Leo Versteegen for helpful comments and suggestions. We also thank both anonymous referees for their careful reading of our paper and for their suggestions, which helped improve the presentation. Open access publishing facilitated by Ecole polytechnique federale de Lausanne, as part of the Wiley - Ecole polytechnique federale de Lausanne agreement via the Consortium Of Swiss Academic Libraries.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonexistence of almost-complete Harary polynomials — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/372265/nonexistence-of-almost-complete-harary-polynomials)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Nonexistence of almost-complete Harary polynomials ... Harary almost-completeness conjecture.\n\nNonexistence of almost-complete Harary polynomials\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $\\mathbb{G}(n,1/2)$G(n,1/2) be the random graph model with edge probability $1/2$1/2, and let a Harary polynomial be a graph polynomial arising from a graph property. A graph polynomial is almost complete when asymptotically almost all graphs in the model are uniquely determined by it.\n\nHarary almost-completeness conjecture. There is no Harary polynomial which is almost complete for $\\mathbb{G}(n,1/2)$G(n,1/2).\n\nThis is proposed in contrast with the Bollobás–Pebody–Riordan conjecture for the chromatic polynomial. The supplied text gives no resolution or supporting theorem for this stronger nonexistence claim.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Johann A. Makowsky, “Distinctive power and comparability of Harary polynomial”, arXiv:2512.22556 (2025).\n\nchromatic-polynomialgraph-polynomialsrandom-graphs\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n\n* * *--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Probability That a Random Graph is Even‐Decomposable (https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/e8fbcbff-df10-4497-9424-255eebb24e46/content)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Our first result is a proof of Conjecture 1.6. ... Using Versteegen’s result (Versteegen 2025) that if \\(H\\) is even-decomposable, then \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\) and Alon’s observation (Alon 2024a) that \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\) if and only if \\(d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n)=n^{-\\Omega(1)}\\), we obtain the following corollary about graph codes and the Ramsey-type problem discussed above. ... On the other hand, he pointed out that even very dense graphs can be even-decomposable and that the method used to prove his Theorem 1.5 is specific to the case \\(p=1/2\\).\nHe also showed that almost all graphs with an even number of edges are even-decomposable. Here and below, \\(G(n,p)\\) denotes the binomial random graph on \\(n\\) vertices with edge probability \\(p\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.5** (Versteegen (2025)). *If \\(H \\sim G(n,\\tfrac12)\\), then*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-non-decomposable}) = e^{-\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\log n})}.\n\\]\n\nVersteegen conjectured the following much stronger bound for the proportion of even-non-decomposable graphs.\n\n**Conjecture 1.6** (Versteegen (2025)). *If \\(H \\sim G(n,\\tfrac12)\\), then*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-non-decomposable}) = e^{-\\Omega(n^2)}.\n\\]\n\nOur first result is a proof of Conjecture 1.6.\n\n**Theorem 1.7.** *If \\(H \\sim G(n,\\tfrac12)\\), then*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-non-decomposable}) = e^{-\\Omega(n^2)}.\n\\]\n\nThis bound is trivially tight up to the value of the implied constant (e.g., since \\(K_4\\) is even-non-decomposable).\n\nUsing Versteegen’s result (Versteegen 2025) that if \\(H\\) is even-decomposable, then \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\) and Alon’s observation (Alon 2024a) that \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\) if and only if \\(d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n)=n^{-\\Omega(1)}\\), we obtain the following corollary about graph codes and the Ramsey-type problem discussed above.\n\n**Corollary 1.8.** *There is a positive absolute constant \\(c\\) such that for all but an at most \\(e^{-cm^2}\\) proportion of \\(m\\)-vertex graphs \\(H\\) with an even number of edges, we have \\(d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n)\\le n^{-\\Omega(1)}\\) and \\(r_H(n)\\ge n^{\\Omega(1)}\\).*\n\nVersteegen noted that, intuitively, the sparser a graph is, the larger its independent sets are, hence the easier it should be to find even-decompositions for it. On the other hand, he pointed out that even very dense graphs can be even-decomposable and that the method used to prove his Theorem 1.5 is specific to the case \\(p=1/2\\). Motivated by these observations, he asked to determine the threshold where even-non-decomposable graphs become dominant.\n\n**Question 1.9** (Versteegen (2025)). *What is the minimal \\(p(n)\\) such that when \\(H \\sim G(n,p(n))\\),*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-non-decomposable}\\mid H \\text{ is even}) \\ge \\frac12?\n\\]\n\nWe answer this question by showing that the threshold is \\(p(n)=1-\\Theta(\\tfrac1n)\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.10.** *There exists an absolute constant \\(c_1>0\\), such that for any \\(p\\le 1-\\frac{c_1}{n}\\) and \\(H\\sim G(n,p)\\), we have*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-non-decomposable})=o(1).\n\\]\n\n**Proposition 1.11.** *There exists an absolute constant \\(c_2>0\\), such that for any \\(p\\ge 1-\\frac{c_2}{n}\\) and \\(H\\sim G(n,p)\\), we have*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(H \\text{ is even-decomposable})=o(1).\n\\]\n\nAlon (2024b) asked whether a random graph also has the following stronger (and slightly more natural) property.\n\n**Definition 1.12** (Even-degenerate graphs). A graph is even-degenerate if there is an ordering \\(v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_n\\) of its vertices such that for each \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\), \\(v_i\\) has an even number of neighbours in the set \\(\\{v_{i+1},\\ldots,v_n\\}\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe always have \\(r_H(n) \\le \\binom{n}{2}\\) by using distinct colours for each edge in \\(K_n\\). Therefore, for any graph \\(H\\), (1) yields the polynomial lower bound (https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/428d0783-a2d0-47c4-9a7b-49b896262665/content)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Versteegen conjectured that being even-decomposable is the only obstruction to a positive answer to Question 1.4/1.5.\n\n**Conjecture 1.8** (Versteegen [13]).\nWe always have \\(r_H(n) \\le \\binom{n}{2}\\) by using distinct colours for each edge in \\(K_n\\). Therefore, for any graph \\(H\\), (1) yields the polynomial lower bound\n\n\\[\nd_H(n) \\ge d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n) = \\Omega(n^{-C_H}),\n\\]\n\nfor some constant \\(C_H > 0\\). It is of key interest to decide for which graphs we have \\(d_H(n) \\ge d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n) = n^{-o(1)}\\).\n\n**Question 1.4.** *For which graphs \\(H\\), with an even number of edges, do we have \\(d_H^{\\mathrm{lin}}(n) = n^{-o(1)}\\)?*\n\nBy virtue of (1), Question 1.4 is equivalent to the following sub-polynomial upper bound for \\(r_H(n)\\).\n\n**Question 1.5.** *For which graphs \\(H\\), with an even number of edges, do we have \\(r_H(n) = n^{o(1)}\\)?*\n\nVersteegen [13] defined a class of *even-decomposable* graphs for which he gave a negative answer to Question 1.4/1.5.\n\n**Definition 1.6.** A graph \\(H\\) with an even number of edges is *even-decomposable* if there is a sequence \\(V(H)=V_0 \\supset V_1 \\supset \\cdots \\supset V_k=\\emptyset\\) such that for each \\(0 \\le i \\le k-1\\), \\(H[V_i]\\) has an even number of edges and \\(V_i \\setminus V_{i+1}\\) is an independent set in \\(H\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.7** (Versteegen [13]). *If \\(H\\) is even-decomposable, then \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\).*\n\nJanzer and Yip [12] showed that the probability that a uniform random graph on \\(t\\) vertices with an even number of edges is even-decomposable is \\(1-e^{-\\Theta(t^2)}\\). Versteegen conjectured that being even-decomposable is the only obstruction to a positive answer to Question 1.4/1.5.\n\n**Conjecture 1.8** (Versteegen [13]). *We have \\(r_H(n)=n^{\\Omega(1)}\\) if and only if \\(H\\) is even-decomposable.*\n\nWe shall focus on the case of cliques \\(H=K_t\\). The requirement that \\(H\\) has an even number of edges is equivalent to \\(t \\equiv 0,1 \\pmod 4\\). It can be seen that for all \\(t \\ge 4\\) satisfying this modular condition, \\(K_t\\) is not even-decomposable. Therefore, Conjecture 1.8 implies that \\(r_{K_t}(n)=n^{o(1)}\\) for any \\(t \\ge 4\\) with \\(t \\equiv 0,1 \\pmod 4\\). This special case of Conjecture 1.8 has been conjectured earlier in [10] by Ge, Xu and Zhang, and remains open.\n\n**Conjecture 1.9** (Ge, Xu and Zhang [10]). *For any positive integer \\(t \\ge 4\\) with \\(t \\equiv 0,1 \\pmod 4\\), we have*\n\n\\[\nr_{K_t}(n)=n^{o(1)}.\n\\]\n\nAffirmative answers were given to Conjecture 1.9 in the cases of \\(t=4\\) (Cameron and Heath [4]) and \\(t=5\\) (Ge, Xu and Zhang [10] and Bennett, Heath and Zerbib [3], independently).\n\n**Theorem 1.10** (Cameron and Heath [4]). *We have*\n\n\\[\nr_{K_4}(n)=e^{O((\\log^{1/2} n)\\log\\log n)}=n^{o(1)}.\n\\]\n\n**Theorem 1.11** (Bennett, Heath and Zerbib [3]; Ge, Xu and Zhang [10]). *We have*\n\n\\[\nr_{K_5}(n)=e^{O((\\log^{1/2} n)\\log\\log n)}=n^{o(1)}.\n\\]\n\nWe remark that Ge, Xu and Zhang obtained the weaker upper bound of \\(r_{K_5}(n)=e^{O((\\log^{2/3} n)\\log\\log n)}\\) and Versteegen [13] showed that the \\(\\log\\log n\\) factor may be removed from the exponent of the upper bound for \\(r_{K_4}(n)\\).\n\nWe give an affirmative answer to Conjecture 1.9 for the smallest open case \\(t=8\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.12.** *We have*\n\n\\[\nr_{K_8}(n)=e^{O(\\log^{2/3} n)}=n^{o(1)}.\n\\]\n\n3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe probability that a random graph is even-decomposable (https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.11152)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Resolving a conjecture of Versteegen, we prove that all but an $e^{-\\Omega(n^2)}$ proportion of the $n$-vertex graphs with an even number of edges are even-decomposable.\nTitle: The probability that a random graph is even-decomposable\nAuthors: Oliver Janzer, Fredy Yip\nDate: Tue Sep 17 13:03:47 2024\n\nA graph $G$ with an even number of edges is called even-decomposable if there is a sequence $V(G)=V_0\\supset V_1\\supset \\dots \\supset V_k=\\emptyset$ such that for each $i$, $G[V_i]$ has an even number of edges and $V_i\\setminus~V_{i+1}$ is an independent set in $G$. The study of this property was initiated recently by Versteegen, motivated by connections to a Ramsey-type problem and questions about graph codes posed by Alon. Resolving a conjecture of Versteegen, we prove that all but an $e^{-\\Omega(n^2)}$ proportion of the $n$-vertex graphs with an even number of edges are even-decomposable. Moreover, answering one of his questions, we determine the order of magnitude of the smallest $p=p(n)$ for which the probability that the random graph $G(n,1-p)$ is even-decomposable (conditional on it having an even number of edges) is at least $1/2$. We also study the following closely related property. A graph is called even-degenerate if there is an ordering $v_1,v_2,\\dots,v_n$ of its vertices such that each $v_i$ has an even number of neighbours in the set $\\{v_{i+1},\\dots,v_n\\}$. We prove that all but an $e^{-\\Omega(n)}$ proportion of the $n$-vertex graphs with an even number of edges are even-degenerate, which is tight up to the implied constant.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n<visual_element id=\